    "title": "Harlingen City Commission to further discuss proposed policies on public comment period - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Gabriela Gonzalez ,\n5 hours ago\nHARLINGEN, Texas ( ValleyCentral ) — The Harlingen City Commission voted unanimously on Wednesday to postpone a city agenda item on whether to adopt new policies for the public comment period at city meetings.\nCity commissioners agreed to take more time to further review a draft and input suggested changes to the proposed policies.\nThe commission agreed to submit their suggestions no later than Friday, July 17.\nA draft of the proposed rules was presented at the previous city meeting held on July 1.\nImages of the proposed draft are available in the images below.\n(Courtesy of the city of Harlingen) (Courtesy of the city of Harlingen) (Courtesy of the city of Harlingen) (Courtesy of the city of Harlingen) (Courtesy of the city of Harlingen) On the July 1 Harlingen city meeting agenda, the policy was proposed for “establishing standards of conduct for public participation and procedures for maintaining order.”\nHarlingen City Commission considers public comment conduct rules ValleyCentral previously spoke with Harlingen Mayor Norma Sepulveda, who said the proposal was created after a recent issue of someone leaving a camera propped on the podium recording for the entirety of public comments.\nThe proposed policy has raised concerns for Harlingen residents, who believe any change would discourage public participation and violate one’s First Amendment rights.\nThis is a developing story.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KVEO-TV.",
    "summary": "City commissioners agreed to take more time to further review a draft and input suggested changes to the proposed policies. The commission agreed to submit their suggestions no later than Friday, July 17. A draft of the proposed rules was presented at the previous city meeting held on July 1. The proposed policy has raised concerns for Harlingen residents, who believe any change would discourage public participation and violate one’s First Amendment rights. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KVEO-TV.",
